# Neon Transit — chipgen v3 demo
# Instruments imported straight from the Furnace OPN library with
# python/furnace_import.py. Render it with:
#
#   python3 python/chipgen.py examples/neon_transit.trk \
#       --bank examples/neon_transit_bank.json \
#       -o neon_transit.wav --vgm neon_transit.vgm --profile
#
# The bank carries three spare patches the score does not use
# (slap_bass_3, techno_bass_1, distorted_guitar_1) so the arrangement
# can be re-voiced by editing one `inst` line.
#
# Channel plan is a real Genesis driver's: FM0-FM4 carry the music and
# FM5 is left to the DAC, because register 0x2B bit 7 hands channel 6
# to the PCM stream outright — a note written there while a drum plays
# does not get quieter, it stops existing. The one place FM5 is used
# is the breakdown, where the drums drop out entirely.

title Neon Transit
author chipgen
game chipgen v3 demo
notes YM2612 + SN76489, instruments from the Furnace OPN library

# 240 ticks/s, not the default 192: at 150 BPM and lpb 4 a row is
# 60/150/4*192 = 19.2 ticks, which rounds to 19 — a prime, so the
# tempo is 1% off AND no grid can subdivide a row for the arpeggios.
# 240 gives a row of exactly 24 ticks, divisible by 3 and 4.
ticks 240
bpm 150
lpb 4

inst fm0 bass_resonant_bass
inst fm1 synth_snes_ish_square
inst fm2 strings_pad
inst fm3 strings_pad
inst fm4 synth_perfect_saw_wave
inst fm5 keys_church_organ

# Stereo: bass and lead centred, the pad pair thrown wide, arp opposite
# the counter-melody. PSG is mono-summed, so width has to come from FM.
pan fm0 C
pan fm1 C
pan fm2 L
pan fm3 R
pan fm4 R
pan fm5 C

mark intro
vol fm2 96
vol fm3 96
cols fm2 fm3 psg0 noise

